 observed_result: [PAP-023] V1(a) 16/23 above p95 binomial p=2.67e-16; V1(b) +0.142 CI[+0.103,+0.187] CONFIRMED; V2a rho=+0.288 p=0.077 CI[-0.244,+0.667]; V2b rho=+0.253 p=0.115 NOT\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:small_sample"}, {"id": "FAM:SF-APPS-009", "score": null, "document": "# FAM:SF-APPS-009 | Study family SF-APPS-009: Player routing V4 vs V5 direct scoring\n- observed_research_question: Player routing V4 vs V5 direct scoring\n- auto_outcome_status: untestable\n- observed_hypothesis: V5 player routing picks players with higher observed PPP than V4 routing.\n- observed_result: 8 divergences, 0 scoreable, 8 unscored (player missing from Neo4j); hit rate n/a.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: A_unanswerable (question not answerable with the data or design)\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:small_sample\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER\n- observed_failure_evidence: Scoreable 0 of 8; V5 mostly routed to 'team' rather than a player.\n- observed_sample_and_power: n=8 divergences, 0 scoreable\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED\n- observed_method: Series where V4 and V5 routed to different players; compare observed PPP (n>=5 each).\n- observed_verdict: inconclusive\n- observed_failure_type: A_unanswerable\n- auto_confidence: high (0.95)"}, {"id": "FAM:SF31", "score": null, "document": "# FAM:SF31 | Study family SF31: Routing prior vs H2H posterior gate diagnostic\n- observed_research_question: Routing prior vs H2H posterior gate diagnostic\n- auto_outcome_status: artifact\n- observed_hypothesis: The n>=15 H2H gate suppresses real player-routing signal.\n- observed_result: [APPM-015] Mean PPP delta lead-default +1.259 (n>=5) to +1.129 (n>=15); low-n leaders are role players with 2-3 PPP on 5-7 possessions.\n- observed_result: [CHT-017] At n=5-9 typical leaders are 1-shot 3.00-PPP role players. Mean PPP delta lead-vs-default +1.26 at n>=5 and +1.13 at n>=15, inflated by small-sample distortion. n>=15 routing-override gate judged to filter nois [cut; read_family for the full text]\n- auto_failure_mechanism: C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:small_sample\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER\n- observed_failure_evidence: [APPM-015] Apparent +1.13 PPP edge is small-sample inflation (e.g. n=6, PPP=3.00).\n- auto_confidence: high (1.0)"}], "n_results": 4}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "offensive reorganization",
    "structural influence (L)",
    "points per possession change",
    "player absence",
    "absence spell",
    "reorganization metric relative to L"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to measure reorganization relative to L without lookahead leakage?",
    "How to isolate the effect of reorganization from confounding factors such as opponent quality and replacement player skill?",
    "Is the sample of 148 absence spells sufficient to detect a plausible effect size?",
    "How to handle regression to the mean in team PPP after an absence?",
    "What is the reliability of the reorganization metric derived from play-type mixes?",
    "Should absence spells be restricted by minimum length or role filter?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF04",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Live trigger timing: Spearman rho -0.2066 between tier and counter PPP (n=557). Early vs late detection mixed; Tier Spearman rho with ROG PPP -0.0457 not significant. Study inconclusive."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF20",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Absence validation: V1 confirmed real absences move structure (16/23 above p95, binomial p=2.67e-16; mean D_real +0.142). V2 LOO simulation did not predict displacement magnitude (rho=+0.288, p=0.077; partial rho=+0.253, p=0.115). Limited by small sample."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-009",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Player routing V4 vs V5: 8 divergences, 0 scoreable (player missing from Neo4j). Question unanswerable; small sample, drift/nonstationarity risk."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF31",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Routing prior vs H2H posterior gate: Mean PPP delta lead-default +1.259 at n>=5 vs +1.129 at n>=15, inflated by small-sample extremes (role players with 2-3 PPP on 5-7 possessions). Gate filters noise but artifact from small n."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF20",
      "what_failed": "Attempt to predict displacement magnitude from pre-absence structural simulation (V2)",
      "why": "Small sample and inadequate signal; rho=+0.288 p=0.077 (FAIL), partial rho=+0.253 p=0.115 (FAIL)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-009",
      "what_failed": "Direct comparison of player routing V4 vs V5",
      "why": "Question unanswerable: 0 of 8 divergences scoreable due to missing player data; LIM:small_sample, RISK:DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF31",
      "what_failed": "Comparison of PPP deltas at low possession thresholds",
      "why": "Artifact from small-sample possession extremes producing inflated leads; low-n leaders are role players with few possessions."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Real player absences cause measurable shifts in team offensive play-type mix (FAM:SF20 V1)",
    "Predicting the magnitude of structural change from pre-absence simulation failed with the available sample (FAM:SF20 V2)",
    "Small-sample possession-based PPP measures produce inflated effect sizes (FAM:SF31)",
    "Trigger-timing strategies have some predictive value for counter effectiveness but do not address reorganization-PPP link (FAM:SF04)"
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether degree of offensive reorganization predicts change in points per possession",
    "That reorganization relative to L has a reliable correlation with efficiency change",
    "That the relationship is strong enough to be detected with 148 spells",
    "That confounders can be adequately controlled in such a design"
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question is novel relative to prior project work; no direct test exists.",
    "The failure of FAM:SF20 V2 suggests that linking structural measures to absence-related outcomes is challenging even for displacement, raising doubts about predicting PPP change.",
    "Operationalizing 'reorganization relative to L' requires a two-stage estimation (L and then residual displacement) which adds noise and reduces power.",
    "Confounders (opponent quality, replacement skill, home/away, regression to mean) are substantial and difficult to fully control with 148 spells.",
    "Many absence spells may show negligible reorganization, reducing the effective sample for correlation."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Exact operationalization of 'reorganization (relative to L)' not standardized; could be D_real / L or residual after accounting for L.",
    "Whether PPP change should be opponent-adjusted and whether it should be per-possession or per-game.",
    "Minimum absence spell length and role filters that balance sample size versus signal clarity.",
    "Stability of L estimates from pre-absence games (sensitivity to number of games and opponent mix).",
    "Potential nonlinear or threshold relationship between reorganization and PPP change."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The failure of FAM:SF20 V2 to predict displacement magnitude from pre-absence structure shows that linking structural metrics to absence outcomes is already difficult. This evidence reduces confidence that reorganization can predict PPP change with the current sample, supporting a decision not to proceed without a larger or better-measured dataset."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.3,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only 148 absence spells; power for detecting a plausible correlation (rho ~0.2) is low, especially with covariates."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Reorganization and L metrics rely on play-type classification with known inferrer limitations, and their reliability under absence is not validated."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "PPP change is confounded by opponent quality, game location, replacement player skill, and other factors difficult to fully control."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "Absences may select for periods with extreme prior performance, causing regression-to-the-mean in PPP unrelated to reorganization."
      },
      {
        "code": "BASE_RATE",
        "text": "Meaningful reorganization may be rare; many absence spells may show negligible structural change, reducing effective sample size."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF20",
      "FAM:SF04",
      "FAM:SF31"
    ],
    "summary": "Prior work established that real absences shift structure (SF20 V1) but failed to predict the magnitude of that shift from simulation (SF20 V2). No study directly tested reorganization predicting PPP change. SF31 warns of small-sample PPP artifact. SF04 studied trigger timing and counters, not reorganization."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "n=148 spells gives limited power for correlation after controls; true effect would need to be large to be detected."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Play-type and structural influence metrics have classification noise that can attenuate true correlations."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Observed PPP change mixes reorganization effect with opponent strength, home/away, replacement quality, and game script."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Absence spells often follow extreme team PPP levels, producing spurious change via regression to the mean."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "If reorganization is measured using any data from the absence period itself without careful separation, leakage may inflate correlation."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Some spells may contain very few team possessions, making PPP change estimates unreliable."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Exploring multiple operationalizations of reorganization without pre-registration raises false positive risk."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.05,
    "untestable": 0.5,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.25
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "Spearman correlation between offensive reorganization magnitude (e.g., residual displacement from pre-absence structure after controlling for L) and change in opponent-adjusted PPP per possession during a player's absence spell (post-absence minus pre-absence).",
    "unit": "Absence spell (player, team, season) meeting: >=5 consecutive missed games; player had >=10 pre-absence games; L computed from those games; team possessions in spell >=50.",
    "sample": "Up to 148 spells from NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular seasons and playoffs, filtered as above.",
    "power": "At true rho=0.20, n=148 yields ~60% power two-tailed alpha=0.05. With 3 covariates, partial correlation power ~40%. Adequate only if true effect rho>0.35.",
    "controls": "Average opponent defensive rating over the spell; home/away proportion; team pre-absence PPP (to address regression to the mean); replacement player pre-absence PPP or RAPM.",
    "falsification": "1) Test correlation in placebo stretches of equal length for the same players when not absent (no reorganization expected); correlation should be near zero. 2) Permute reorganization values across spells; correlation should vanish.",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-register one primary reorganization metric and one Spearman correlation. Any secondary metrics reported with Bonferroni correction.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject null if two-sided p < 0.05; report confidence interval. Do not interpret marginal significance as evidence.",
    "data_version": "Fixed NBA stats API snapshot dated 2026-11-01, with play-type classifier version frozen and documented."
  }
}}

